<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
  <meta name="description" content="Find out what could make work easier, and ask for it. No form, no login, and no need to say what condition you have."/>
  <title>Curb Cut</title>
</head>
<body>
<c:CurbCutShell pageTitle="Curb Cut" current="home"
  eyebrow="Accommodation without disclosure"
  heading="Find out what could make work easier. Then ask for it."
  lede="No form. No login. And you never have to say what condition you have.">

  <section>
    <div class="plain">
      <span class="tag">In one sentence</span>
      <p>
        Text a number, say what is hard at work, and get real answers about what
        you could ask for &mdash; anonymously, if you want. If you decide to ask
        for something, nothing is sent until you say yes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Sixty-one in a hundred cost nothing</h2>
    <p>
      The Job Accommodation Network asked 5,406 employers what accommodations
      actually cost, between January 2019 and December 2024.
      <strong>61 out of every 100 cost the employer nothing at all.</strong>
      Of the rest, the middle cost was about $300, once.
    </p>
    <p>
      And yet in 2022, Statistics Canada found that
      <strong>more than a third of employed Canadians with a disability had an
      accommodation need that went unmet</strong>. The most common thing people
      needed was not equipment. It was modified working hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      The money was never the barrier. The process is. To get help you first have
      to find the policy, learn its words, hold a work login, fill in a form, and
      tell a stranger something you may not want to tell anyone.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a lot to ask of someone who is already having a hard week.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What is different here</h2>

    <h3>You can ask before you tell anyone anything</h3>
    <p>
      You can find out what you could ask for without giving your name, and
      without saying what condition you have. Nothing is written down while you
      are anonymous. Knowing is allowed. Asking is optional.
    </p>

    <h3>There is nowhere to put a diagnosis</h3>
    <p>
      This is not a promise to be discreet. There is no field for a diagnosis,
      a condition, a disability type, or a medical note anywhere in the system.
      If you mention one, it is not recorded, and you will be told so. A manager
      who asks is refused, because there is genuinely nothing to tell them.
    </p>

    <h3>Nothing is sent until you say yes</h3>
    <p>
      A request is written for you, in your words, and read back. It is not sent
      until you clearly agree. &ldquo;I guess so&rdquo; is not agreement, and you
      will be asked again.
    </p>

    <h3>You will never be told to phone someone</h3>
    <p>
      Every workplace process seems to end at &ldquo;give us a call.&rdquo; If you
      cannot use a phone, that is a locked door. Here, a real person always
      reaches you by message.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>However you want to talk</h2>
    <p>
      Text, a voice note, a video, or a photo of your desk. All four are front
      doors, not fallbacks, and you get an answer back the same way you asked.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Sign language goes to a human being.</strong> Signed video is
        never machine translated. It is passed to a person, and you get a text
        straight away so you are not left waiting in silence, wondering.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>This is not a new idea. It is an unfinished one.</h2>
    <p>
      The Government of Canada already built this principle. Its Workplace
      Accessibility Passport records what someone needs once, belongs to the
      employee rather than the employer, and moves with them across the federal
      public service, so nobody has to justify themselves again to each new
      manager.
    </p>
    <p>
      It works. It is also only available if you happen to work for the federal
      public service. Curb Cut takes the same principle to everyone else &mdash;
      including contractors, and applicants who do not have a work account at all
      and are currently expected to disclose a disability to a stranger before
      they have even been hired.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>If you want to try it</h2>
    <p>
      Text <span class="kw">+1 276 495 9311</span> and say what is hard.
      That is the whole thing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Message and data rates may apply. Reply <span class="kw">HELP</span> for
      help, or <span class="kw">STOP</span> to stop receiving messages.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>This page, too</h2>
    <p>
      A site arguing that process shuts people out should not shut anyone out.
      Text size and contrast controls are at the top and remember your choice.
      Nothing here relies on colour alone to tell you something. It works by
      keyboard, it works with a screen reader, and it holds still if you have
      asked your device to reduce motion.
    </p>
  </section>

</c:CurbCutShell>
</body>
</html>
